  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "N.Kamaraj vs The Food Safety Officer on 10 June, 2024 Crl.O.P.(MD) N Before The Madurai Bench Of Madras High Court Dated: 10.06.2024 Coram The Honourable Mr. Justice A.A.Nakkiran Crl.O.P (Md) No.2865 of 2021 and Crl.M.P(MD) Nos.1529 & 1530 of 2021 1. N.Kamaraj 2. M.Prabhakaran ... Petitioners/Accused N Vs The Food Safety Officer, Madurai Corporation ( Code No.572), Food Safety and Drug Administration Department, Viswanathapuram, Madurai-625 014. ... Respondent /Complainant PRAYER:",
    "facts": "This Criminal Original Petition has been filed to quash the proceedings in S.T.C.No.3550 of 2020 on the file of the Judicial Magistrate Court No.1, Madurai. The learned counsel for the petitioners submitted that the complainant Designated Officer along with the Food Safety Officers came to the shop of the first accused viz., Kamaraj, on 05.02.2020, at about 12.10 p.m., and took food samples of mr;R bty;yk;; (Loose Condition) and forwarded it to the Food Analyst. He further submitted that the first accused had stated that he is the retailer and the designated officer seems to have taken 4 samples weighing 250 kg each from the first accused and sealed the same by following the procedure as contemplated under the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006, and seized the remaining back stock of the said mr;R bty;yk;; (Loose Condition) and had sent the same to the Food Analyst. The Food Analyst had sent a report on 11.03.2020 stating that the sample was substandard and unsafe. The Designated Officer had sent the report of the Food Analyst along with his notice, dated 20.03.2020. Based on the report of the Food Analysis, a detailed report was submitted to the https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis Commissioner, Food Safety and Drug Administration, Chennai, sought for permission to initiate proceedings against the accused. Since there was no claim from the petitioners to sent the second sample for analyzing, a complaint was lodged before the learned Judicial Magistrate Court No.I, Madurai as per Section 190(1)(a) and 200 of Cr.P.C., against the accused for the offences punishable under Sections 51, 59(i) of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 and the same was taken on file in S.T.C.No.3550 of 2020. Aggrieved against the same, this Criminal original Petition is filed to quash the proceedings as against the petitioners. The Designated Officer, Madurai has sent a notice to the accused on 20.03.2020 and the same was received by the accused on 21.03.2020. Due to Covid-19 pandemic, the Government has announced lock down from 23.03.2020 and there was no opportunity to the petitioners to file an appeal. Accordingly, he prayed to quash the impugned charge sheet. https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis 4.Per contra, the learned Additional Public Prosecutor appearing for the first respondent submitted that the recommendation letter was sent to the petitioners on 19.03.2020. But, on the same day itself, they sent a communication to the respondent on 19.03.2020 stating that they have not want to send second sample for food analyzing. Based on that only, the present proceedings have been initiated against the petitioners.",
    "arguments": "3.The learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submitted that the petitioners herein are only retailer. He further submitted that there are materials available to proceed with the case as against the petitioners herein and at the threshold, the criminal proceedings cannot be [LEAKAGE_MASK] and the charges against the petitioners have to be gone into only at the time of trial and hence, he prayed for dismissal of the petition. 5.Heard the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner and the learned Additional Public Prosecutor appearing for the respondent. 6.Time and again, this Court as well as the Supreme Court has cautioned the courts about the necessary precautions to be taken while quashing the charge sheet at the initial stage. Useful reference in this regard can be had to the decision of the Honble Apex Court in State of Haryana Vs - Bhajan Lal (1992 SCC (Crl.) 426). N.Kamaraj vs The Food Safety Officer on 10 June, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/175089007/ 2 https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3.The learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submitted that the petitioners herein are only retailer. He further submitted that there are materials available to proceed with the case as against the petitioners herein and at the threshold, the criminal proceedings cannot be [LEAKAGE_MASK] and the charges against the petitioners have to be gone into only at the time of trial and hence, he prayed for dismissal of the petition. 5.Heard the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner and the learned Additional Public Prosecutor appearing for the respondent. 6.Time and again, this Court as well as the Supreme Court has cautioned the courts about the necessary precautions to be taken while quashing the charge sheet at the initial stage.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
